POSTS_EN = {
"reclamo-contra-banco-ley-del-consumidor": {
 "titulo": "When your bank fails you: how to file a claim before the Local Police Court",
 "titulo_seo": "Claims against banks under Chile's Consumer Law",
 "desc": "What to do if your bank charged you without authorisation or withheld your funds in Chile: where to claim, what to ask for, deadlines and documents.",
 "html": """<p>A charge you never authorised. Funds the bank withholds without explanation. An answer that never comes, or that contradicts what you were told at the branch. These situations are common, and many people believe the only way out is to accept whatever the bank offers. That is not so: Chile's Consumer Protection Law (Law 19,496) also applies to banks and gives customers a concrete way to claim.</p>
<h2>Where is the claim filed?</h2>
<p>Individual claims for breaches of this law are filed before the Local Police Court (Juzgado de Policía Local). The law lets the consumer choose between the court of their own domicile or that of the supplier's domicile. In practice, this means you can sue the bank in your own municipality, without having to litigate where its head office is.</p>
<h2>What can you ask for?</h2>
<p>The same proceeding pursues two different things. First, a declaration that the bank breached the law and a fine against it. Second, an order to compensate the damage you suffered: the money lost and, where appropriate, other harm. What is available in each case depends on the facts and, above all, on the evidence.</p>
<h2>Must you complain to SERNAC first?</h2>
<p>No. A complaint to SERNAC, or to the bank's customer service, is voluntary. It has one advantage: while it is being processed, the limitation period is suspended. But it is not a requirement to go to court.</p>
<h2>How long do you have?</h2>
<p>The action to pursue the breach is time-barred two years after the breach ceased. Even so, it is best not to wait: records, emails and witnesses are lost over time.</p>
<h2>Do you need a lawyer?</h2>
<p>The law allows the complaint and claim before the Local Police Court to be filed without a lawyer. However, when the amount is significant, when heirs are involved or when the bank disputes that you are a “consumer”, legal advice helps present the case properly and avoid losing remedies through short deadlines.</p>
<h2>Before filing, gather</h2>
<ul><li>Statements and receipts for the disputed transactions.</li><li>Emails, letters and complaint numbers with the bank.</li><li>The date you learned of the problem and what the bank answered.</li><li>Any document the bank asked you for and the date you delivered it.</li></ul>
<p>At Salinas &amp; Cía. Abogados we review your case and tell you frankly whether the consumer route is the right one or whether another action is advisable.</p>""",
 "fuentes": [("Law 19,496 (official text, Library of Congress of Chile)", "https://www.bcn.cl/leychile/navegar?idNorma=61438")]},
"protocolo-familiar-empresa-familiar": {
 "titulo": "Family protocol: how to protect the business before succession",
 "titulo_seo": "Family protocol: protect your family business",
 "desc": "What a family protocol is, what it governs and why it must be reflected in bylaws, shareholder agreements and wills before succession, under Chilean law.",
 "html": """<p>Many family businesses run well while the founder is in charge. The problems come later: deciding who will lead, what happens to the shares of a child who does not work in the company, or how to divide the inheritance without splitting the business. The family protocol is the tool to answer those questions before they become disputes.</p>
<h2>What is a family protocol?</h2>
<p>It is an agreement among the members of a business family that sets the rules of their relationship with the company. In Chile it has no specific legal regulation: it is a private agreement, and its strength depends on how it is drafted and on its rules also being carried into the documents the law does recognise.</p>
<h2>What does it usually cover?</h2>
<ul><li>Which family members may work in the company and on what conditions.</li><li>How directors or managers are chosen.</li><li>What happens if a partner wants to sell: family pre-emption rights and how shares are valued.</li><li>How profits are distributed and when they are reinvested.</li><li>How disagreements are resolved, for example through a family council or arbitration.</li><li>How and when management passes to the next generation.</li></ul>
<h2>The protocol alone is not enough</h2>
<p>For its rules to be enforceable, they must be reflected in other instruments: the company's bylaws, shareholder agreements and, for succession, wills. The latter requires care, because Chilean law reserves part of the estate for forced heirs: the founder cannot freely dispose of all their assets, and planning must respect those limits.</p>
<h2>When should it be done?</h2>
<p>While the founder is active and the family is on good terms. A protocol negotiated in advance, with all branches of the family involved, is far more likely to be followed than one improvised in the middle of a dispute.</p>
<h2>Three signs your company needs one</h2>
<ol><li>There are children or nephews and nieces with different expectations about the company.</li><li>The bylaws do not say what happens to the shares if a partner dies or wants to leave.</li><li>Important decisions depend on a single person.</li></ol>
<p>At Salinas &amp; Cía. Abogados we support business families in drafting the protocol and implementing it in bylaws, agreements and estate planning.</p>""",
 "fuentes": [("Civil Code, arts. 1167 et seq. and 1184; Law 18,046, art. 14 [CONFIRMAR referencias]", None)]},
}
